<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre Buyoya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a>–<a href="/years/2020">2020</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre Buyoya was a Tutsi army officer who twice seized power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a>, ruling from 1987 to 1993 and again from 1996 to 2003. In his first term he opened the way to multiparty elections, which he lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melchior-ndadaye">Melchior Ndadaye</a> in 1993. His second coup came during the civil war, and he later took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arusha">Arusha</a> peace process.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-buyoya.jpg" alt="Pierre Buyoya" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chatham House · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pierre_Buyoya_at_Chatham_House_2013_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pierre Buyoya was born in Rutovu, Bururi Province, on 24 November 1949, in Belgian-administered Ruanda-Urundi. His father, Rurikumunwa, was a Tutsi-Hima of the Batyaba clan. Buyoya was not a family surname but a descriptive name that can be translated as baby: his parents had lost several other children and feared he too might die young, and the name was never changed.</p><p>He was educated at a Catholic mission in Rutovu and then at the Ecole moyenne pédagogique, before enlisting as an officer and studying at the Royal Military Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, at the General Staff College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and at the Bundeswehr Command and Staff College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>. He joined the single party, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-for-national-progress">Union for National Progress</a> (UPRONA), and sat on its Central Committee from 1979.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In September 1987 Buyoya led a coup against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-bagaza">Jean-Baptiste Bagaza</a> and chaired a 31-person military committee. In August 1988 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> uprising in the communes of Ntega and Marangara killed about 300 Tutsi, and the army retaliated by killing approximately 20,000 Hutu. Buyoya then appointed a Commission of National Reconciliation, named the Hutu Adrien Sibomana as prime minister and allowed public debate on policy.</p><p>A Charter of Unity was approved by referendum in 1991, and a new constitution of 1992 provided for a multi-party system. The researcher Nigel Watt judged the unity measures cosmetic. Buyoya lost the June 1993 election to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melchior-ndadaye">Melchior Ndadaye</a>, who was assassinated in an attempted army coup in October 1993. Some human rights groups suspected Buyoya of supporting the plotters, and the civil war that followed killed 300,000 people.</p><p>On 25 July 1996 Buyoya returned to power in a coup that ousted interim President Sylvestre Ntibantunganya. International economic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> were eased as he formed an ethnically inclusive government and, in June 1998, a partnership with the National Assembly, dominated by the Hutu-backed FRODEBU. This led to the Arusha Accords of 2000, which introduced ethnic power-sharing. He chose a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a>, Domitien Ndayizeye, as vice-president and handed power to him on 30 April 2003.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the war Buyoya became a senator for life under the 2004 constitution and led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> peace missions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, from 2008, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. In his 2007 book the former ambassador Robert Krueger accused him of orchestrating the 1993 putsch. On 19 October 2020 the Supreme Court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a> sentenced him in absentia to life imprisonment for the murder of Ndadaye. He contracted COVID-19 in Mali and died on 17 December 2020 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a>. In July 2024 his remains were returned to Burundi and buried in Rutovu.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pierre Buyoya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Further coups brought Jean-Baptiste Bagaza in 1976 and <b>Pierre Buyoya</b> in 1987.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-burundi">Republic of Burundi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rutovu Commune, in what is now <a href="/countries/burundi">Burundi</a>. Died in Bonneuil-en-France, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-3.88,29.85&amp;year=1995" aria-label="Rutovu Commune on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pierre Buyoya"><image href="/maps/globes/g03000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4987 5332h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4987 5332h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-3.88,29.85&amp;year=1995">What was here in 1995? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
